<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Metadata for the recently added widget, read by the launcher before any of our
  code runs.

  ## Sizes

  design/html/15-Widget.html draws this card as a 166dp square on a 390dp phone;
  design/html/18-TabletWidget.html draws the same card at 200dp. One fluid
  composition serves both — RecentlyAddedWidget uses SizeMode.Exact and reads the
  real size from LocalSize — so the numbers below describe a square cell block
  rather than a breakpoint.

  The conventional translation from grid cells to dp is `70 * n - 30`, which puts
  166dp between two cells (110dp) and three (180dp). The card is declared as 2x2
  because that is what a square glanceable widget is on every launcher's grid,
  and the dp minima below are stated as the floor at which the card can still
  draw what it is for rather than as a cell count:

    * minWidth 140dp — the 56dp cover, the 40dp Play disc and 28dp of padding
      leave 16dp for the album title at anything narrower, which is not a title.
      Below WidgetSquareDimensions.playButtonMinWidth (150dp) the disc drops
      first and the title gets its 40dp back, so the card degrades rather than
      breaking at the boundary.
    * minHeight 140dp — 56dp of cover, 28dp of padding and three text lines is
      about 130dp. Below WidgetSquareDimensions.subtitleMinHeight (130dp) the
      artist line drops, which is the one of the three lines a person can infer.
    * minResize* are lower than both, because a user resizing a widget below
      what it advertises gets a card that has already been designed to shed
      content in a stated order rather than one that clips.
    * maxResize* are generous but not unlimited: nothing in the design grows
      usefully past about twice its drawn size, and a 400dp-tall card headed
      "Recently added" would be one cover and a great deal of nothing.

  ## updatePeriodMillis is 0, deliberately

  The platform clamps this to 30 minutes and wakes the device to honour it. The
  newest album changes when a library sync writes one and at no other time, so a
  timer would be a battery cost paid to redraw an identical card. While the app's
  process is alive the Glance session recomposes from the Room query; when it is
  not, the redraw is pushed by NeedlerWidgets.refreshRecentlyAdded from whoever
  finished the sync. See RecentlyAddedWidget's KDoc.

  ## previewLayout

  widget_recently_added_preview.xml, which draws the empty state. The Android 12+
  picker inflates a preview in its own process with no Hilt graph and no app
  process running, so it cannot be the real card; drawing the empty state means
  the picture is the one a user actually gets on a fresh install, rather than a
  promise about an album this device may not have. initialLayout is Glance's own
  loading layout, shown for the few milliseconds before the first composition
  arrives.
-->
<appwidget-provider xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:description="@string/widget_recently_added_description"
    android:initialLayout="@layout/glance_default_loading_layout"
    android:minWidth="140dp"
    android:minHeight="140dp"
    android:minResizeWidth="110dp"
    android:minResizeHeight="110dp"
    android:maxResizeWidth="320dp"
    android:maxResizeHeight="320dp"
    android:previewLayout="@layout/widget_recently_added_preview"
    android:resizeMode="horizontal|vertical"
    android:targetCellWidth="2"
    android:targetCellHeight="2"
    android:updatePeriodMillis="0"
    android:widgetCategory="home_screen" />
